---
import fig1 from '../assets/figures/fig1-escort-problem.webp';
import fig3a from '../assets/figures/fig3a-success.webp';
import fig3b from '../assets/figures/fig3b-length.webp';
import fig4 from '../assets/figures/fig4-runtime.webp';
import fig5a from '../assets/figures/fig5a-ablation-success.webp';
import fig5b from '../assets/figures/fig5b-ablation-length.webp';
import fig6 from '../assets/figures/fig6-ablation-runtime.webp';
import fig7 from '../assets/figures/fig7-holoocean.webp';
import fig8 from '../assets/figures/fig8-monte-carlo.webp';
import Citation from '../components/Citation.astro';
import ConstraintLadder from '../components/ConstraintLadder.astro';
import Disclosure from '../components/Disclosure.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import FigurePanels from '../components/FigurePanels.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import MathBlock from '../components/MathBlock.astro';
import ResultsTable from '../components/ResultsTable.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import SectionNav from '../components/SectionNav.astro';
import StatTile from '../components/StatTile.astro';
import Tabs from '../components/Tabs.astro';
import VideoEmbed from '../components/VideoEmbed.astro';
import { project, videoPlanningStart, youtubeId } from '../config';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { warnPlaceholders } from '../lib/placeholders';

warnPlaceholders();

// Problem (6) of the paper.
const problem = String.raw`\begin{aligned}
\min_{\{\theta_i(\cdot)\},\,S_1}\quad & S_1 \\
\text{s.t.}\quad & \det\big(\Sigma_1(S_1)\big)^{1/4} \le \bar u \\
& a_j^{\top} x_i(s) \ge b_j + r_a + z_\delta \sqrt{a_j^{\top}\big(\Sigma_i(s) + \Sigma_o\big)\,a_j} \\
& |\kappa_i(s)| \le 1/r_{\min} \\
& x_i(0) = x_i^0,\quad \Sigma_i(0) = \Sigma^0,\quad x_1(S_1) = G
\end{aligned}`;

// Bodies are trusted markup for sub- and superscripts.
const constraints = [
  {
    title: 'Terminal uncertainty',
    body: 'The primary must arrive with det(Σ)<sup>1/4</sup> ≤ <i>ū</i>, the standard deviation of an isotropic covariance of the same determinant.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Chance-constrained clearance',
    body: 'Every agent clears every obstacle with probability at least 1 − <i>δ</i> at every instant. The margin covers the vehicle radius and the position uncertainty of both agent and obstacle.',
  },
  { title: 'Turn radius', body: 'Each trajectory keeps its curvature within 1/<i>r</i><sub>min</sub>.' },
  {
    title: 'Boundary conditions',
    body: 'Agents start from known positions with a common prior covariance, and the primary must end at the goal <i>G</i>.',
  },
];

const belief = [
  {
    title: 'Dead reckoning',
    body: 'Between measurements, position variance grows linearly with distance, faster along the direction of travel than across it.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Landmark fixes',
    body: 'Range-bearing measurements whose weight decays smoothly with distance through a logistic taper, with no hard cutoff.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Covariance intersection',
    body: 'At fixed arc-length intervals, agents exchange estimates and fuse them by covariance intersection, which stays consistent under unknown cross-correlation.',
  },
];

// How each baseline plans (Sec. V-B; code/src/planner/algorithms).
const baselines = [
  { name: 'Greedy', body: 'The primary takes its shortest route; the support takes whichever next step most reduces the primary’s uncertainty.' },
  { name: 'Formation', body: 'The primary plans alone while the support holds a fixed position beside it.' },
  { name: 'Sequential', body: 'Agents plan one at a time: the primary, then the support against it, then the primary once more.' },
  { name: 'CL-GBT', body: 'A sampling-based tree over both agents’ joint state, after Theurkauf et al., instead of a lattice search.' },
];
---

<Base>
  <Hero next="video" />
  <SectionNav
    after="top"
    items={[
      { id: 'video', label: 'Video' },
      { id: 'overview', label: 'Overview' },
      { id: 'abstract', label: 'Abstract' },
      { id: 'method', label: 'Method' },
      { id: 'constraint-ladder', label: 'Constraint ladder' },
      { id: 'results', label: 'Results' },
      { id: 'simulation', label: 'HoloOcean' },
      { id: 'bibtex', label: 'BibTeX' },
    ]}
  />

  <Section id="video" tint>
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
      <VideoEmbed id={youtubeId} title={`${project.name} video`} />
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="overview">
    <Figure
      src={fig1}
      alt="Hexagonal lattice with an obstacle in the middle. The primary agent's path passes above the obstacle to the goal; the support agent's path dips below it toward a landmark, takes a reading, and relays position data to the primary near the goal."
      label="The escort problem."
      class="mx-auto max-w-[36rem]"
    >
      The primary must reach the goal with its localization uncertainty under a terminal bound, and the support is routed
      to hold it there. The <span class="font-medium text-support-agent">support agent</span>{' '}
      <span class="font-medium text-landmark-ink">localizes with a landmark</span> and{' '}
      <span class="font-medium text-relay">relays position data</span>, enabling the{' '}
      <span class="font-medium text-primary-agent">primary agent</span> to take a more direct path.
    </Figure>
  </Section>

  <Section id="abstract" title="Abstract" width="prose" tint>
    <div class="prose-body text-justify hyphens-auto">
      <p>
        Centralized multi-agent path planning with cooperative localization in GPS-denied environments is generally
        computationally intractable when formulated in full belief space due to tightly coupled uncertainty dynamics.
        Prior work typically approximates belief dynamics or restricts inter-agent coupling. This work examines a
        multi-agent planning problem in which a primary agent must reach a goal in minimum time subject to a
        chance-constrained collision-avoidance requirement and a terminal bound on localization uncertainty. We obtain a
        tractable surrogate of the underlying belief-space problem by optimizing over the mean-state trajectories of all
        agents and propagating covariance for terminal constraint evaluation only. We propose <em>CONSORT</em> (Cooperative
        Navigation with Support-Optimized Routing and Trajectories), a discrete-to-continuous pipeline combining
        hexagonal-grid joint search with spline-based trajectory refinement. Across 50 randomized scenarios, the planners
        separate as the terminal uncertainty bound tightens, with <em>CONSORT</em> solving <strong>80% of scenarios</strong>{' '}
        where the strongest baseline solves 54%, at comparable path length. In marine simulations, with the plans executed
        under feedback control on an independently developed factor-graph estimate, the trajectories remain collision-free
        and their terminal uncertainty falls <strong>within 6% of the planner’s prediction</strong>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="method"
    title="Method"
    lead="Optimize the mean trajectories of the whole team, and propagate covariance along them only to check the constraints."
  >
    <div class="grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-white p-6">
        <p class="font-display text-sm font-medium tracking-wider text-accent uppercase">Stage 1</p>
        <p class="mt-1 font-display text-xl font-medium text-ink">Joint search on a hexagonal lattice</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          Each graph node pairs a hexagonal cell with one of six headings, and three moves leave it: straight, or a ±60°
          turn into the cell faced by the new heading, the lattice’s counterpart of the turn-radius bound. An A*-style
          search advances every agent one edge per expansion, propagates each covariance along its edge, and fuses them at
          communication checkpoints. Pareto pruning over path length and covariance keeps the joint space manageable, and
          the search is anytime with respect to the terminal bound.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-white p-6">
        <p class="font-display text-sm font-medium tracking-wider text-accent uppercase">Stage 2</p>
        <p class="mt-1 font-display text-xl font-medium text-ink">Continuous B-spline refinement</p>
        <p class="mt-3 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
          The seed becomes clamped cubic B-splines with its cell centers as control points. Each segment is enclosed in its
          MINVO hull, which makes obstacle clearance linear in the control points. A log-barrier merit function, minimized
          with Adam, shortens the primary’s path while keeping every constraint satisfied, and the same objective pulls an
          infeasible seed toward feasibility. Refinement cuts the lattice’s zigzag corners, shortening the seed by up to 7%.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    {
      youtubeId && (
        <p class="mt-4 text-center text-[0.95rem] text-ink-2">
          <a href={`https://youtu.be/${youtubeId}?t=${videoPlanningStart}`} data-video-seek={videoPlanningStart} class="link">
            See both stages animated in the video
          </a>{' '}
          <span class="text-ink-3">({Math.floor(videoPlanningStart / 60)}:{String(videoPlanningStart % 60).padStart(2, '0')})</span>
        </p>
      )
    }

    <Disclosure summary="Problem formulation and belief model" class="mt-4">
      <div class="prose-body">
        <p>
          The joint belief of a team grows exponentially with its size, and inter-agent measurements couple the agents’
          beliefs. CONSORT therefore takes as decision variables only the heading profile of every agent and the primary’s
          path length <span class="font-serif text-[1.1em] italic">S</span><sub>1</sub>. All agents move at one constant
          speed, so arc length is a shared clock and minimum time means minimum length:
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-5 rounded-xl border border-line bg-tint px-4 py-5 sm:px-6">
        <MathBlock tex={problem} />
      </div>
      <h4 class="mt-7 font-display text-sm font-medium tracking-wider text-accent uppercase">Constraints</h4>
      <dl class="mt-3 grid gap-x-8 gap-y-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
        {
          constraints.map((c) => (
            <div>
              <dt class="font-display font-medium text-ink">{c.title}</dt>
              <dd class="mt-1 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2" set:html={c.body} />
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
      <h4 class="mt-8 font-display text-sm font-medium tracking-wider text-accent uppercase">Belief model</h4>
      <p class="mt-1 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">
        Covariance is not a decision variable. It is a deterministic function of the mean trajectories, built from three
        parts:
      </p>
      <dl class="mt-4 grid gap-x-8 gap-y-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
        {
          belief.map((b) => (
            <div>
              <dt class="font-display font-medium text-ink">{b.title}</dt>
              <dd class="mt-1 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-2">{b.body}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>
    </Disclosure>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="constraint-ladder"
    title="The constraint ladder"
    lead="One scenario, four terminal bounds. Every tightening is paid for in primary path length."
    tint
  >
    <ConstraintLadder />
    <p class="mx-auto mt-5 max-w-3xl text-center text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-3">
      The four plans of Fig. 2, replayed from the planner’s runs: a 1700 m corridor with 14 obstacles and three landmarks.
      Green links are the planner’s exchanges, scheduled every 100 m of arc and faded by its weight. The weight halves at
      the 300 m communication range but has no hard cutoff, so an exchange beyond the range still counts, at reduced
      weight, and is drawn dashed. Orange lines are landmark readings. The faint ring, the primary’s communication range,
      appears as the support nears its edge.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="results"
    title="Benchmark results"
    lead="50 randomized scenarios with one primary and one support, as the terminal bound tightens from 100% to 30% of the single-agent reference."
  >
    <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
      <StatTile value="80%" aside=" vs. 54%" label="of scenarios solved at half the reference bound, against the strongest baseline" />
      <StatTile value="1.14–1.41×" label="mean primary path length relative to the single-agent reference, from the loosest bound to the tightest" />
      <StatTile value="18 s" label="median wall-clock time per run, against 14 s for Formation and Sequential" />
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10">
      <Tabs
        label="Benchmark results"
        tabs={[
          { id: 'success', label: 'Success rate & length' },
          { id: 'table', label: 'Path length table' },
          { id: 'ablation', label: 'Ablation' },
          { id: 'time', label: 'Planning time' },
        ]}
      >
        <div role="tabpanel" id="panel-success" aria-labelledby="tab-success">
          <FigurePanels
            panels={[
              { src: fig3a, alt: 'Success rate versus constraint level for CONSORT and four baselines.' },
              { src: fig3b, alt: 'Mean primary path length over the solved scenarios, relative to the single-agent reference, versus constraint level.' },
            ]}
            label="Fig. 3."
          >
            Fraction of the 50 scenarios solved (left) and mean primary path length over the solved scenarios, normalized by
            the single-agent reference length (right), as the bound tightens.
          </FigurePanels>
        </div>
        <div role="tabpanel" id="panel-table" aria-labelledby="tab-table" hidden>
          <ResultsTable />
          <p class="mt-3 text-[0.925rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-3">
            <span class="font-semibold text-ink-2">Table I.</span> Mean ± SD of primary path length over each method’s own
            solved scenarios, normalized by <span class="font-serif italic">L</span><sub>ref</sub>. Entries are not comparable
            across methods without the success rates, since a method that solves only its easiest scenarios reports a short
            path. † marks fewer than five solved scenarios; “–” means none solved.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div role="tabpanel" id="panel-ablation" aria-labelledby="tab-ablation" hidden>
          <FigurePanels
            panels={[
              { src: fig5a, alt: 'Ablation: success rate of the full pipeline, the discrete stage alone, and a straight-line seed.' },
              { src: fig5b, alt: 'Ablation: mean primary path length of the three arms, relative to the single-agent reference.' },
            ]}
            label="Fig. 5."
          >
            Ablation on a separate set of 30 scenarios drawn without the straight-line screen: success rate (left) and mean
            primary path length over the solved scenarios (right).
          </FigurePanels>
          <p class="mx-auto mt-6 max-w-3xl text-center text-ink-2">
            The joint search finds the landmarks, and the refinement makes the route short: the discrete stage alone returns
            paths 2 to 7% longer at every level down to 50%, and a straight seed solves none below 60%.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div role="tabpanel" id="panel-time" aria-labelledby="tab-time" hidden>
          <div class="grid gap-x-6 gap-y-3 md:grid-cols-2">
            <Figure subgrid src={fig4} alt="Box plots of wall-clock time per planner at five constraint levels, on a log scale." label="Fig. 4.">
              Wall-clock time per planner over successful runs (log scale). Most of it is overhead shared with the
              single-agent reference, which takes 16 s; only CL-GBT grows with the bound, and its longest run took 730 s.
            </Figure>
            <Figure subgrid class="max-md:mt-6" src={fig6} alt="Box plots of wall-clock time of the three ablation arms." label="Fig. 6.">
              Wall-clock time of the ablation arms. The refinement adds about 1.5 s to the discrete stage.
            </Figure>
          </div>
        </div>
      </Tabs>
    </div>

    <h3 class="mt-16 text-center font-serif text-[1.9rem] text-ink">Baselines</h3>
    <p class="mx-auto mt-2 max-w-2xl text-center text-ink-2">
      All four share CONSORT’s covariance model, obstacle test, and refinement. They differ in how they route the team.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
      {
        baselines.map((b) => (
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-white p-5">
            <p class="font-display font-medium text-ink">{b.name}</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-2">{b.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="simulation"
    title="Validation in HoloOcean"
    lead="One plan, executed 30 times with a vehicle, controller, and estimator that share none of the planner’s equations."
    tint
  >
    <div class="grid items-start gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
      <Figure src={fig7} alt="Two torpedo-shaped autonomous underwater vehicles over the seabed in the HoloOcean simulator." label="Fig. 7.">
        The two vehicles in HoloOcean, each following its planned spline at a fixed depth over the seabed.
      </Figure>
      <Figure src={fig8} alt="Planned and mean executed trajectories of both agents with three-sigma cross-track envelopes around a wall." label="Fig. 8.">
        Closed-loop Monte Carlo of the behind-wall plan, with each agent’s mean executed trajectory and ±3σ cross-track
        envelope. The primary passes north of the wall while the support diverts south to the landmark and rejoins before
        the goal.
      </Figure>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
      <StatTile value="30 / 30" label="runs reached the goal within 2% of the planned length, and none penetrated the wall" />
      <StatTile value="1.70 m" aside=" vs. 1.80 m" label="terminal σ measured from the true error, against the planner’s prediction" />
      <StatTile value="28 / 30" label="runs with the estimator’s terminal marginal under the 1.8 m bound" />
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto mt-8 max-w-3xl prose-body">
      <p>
        Every planner in the benchmark shares one covariance model, so the benchmark cannot tell whether that model is
        calibrated. Here torpedo-shaped vehicles follow the plan under HoloOcean 2.0’s depth and heading autopilot, steering on
        an independently developed GTSAM factor-graph estimate, with acoustic exchanges every 100 m that succeed only within
        the planner’s communication range. The true terminal error came within 6% of the prediction, on the conservative
        side, though it also carried a 2.6 m along-track bias that no covariance check captures.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="bibtex" title="BibTeX">
    <Citation />
  </Section>

  <Footer />
</Base>
